From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MIRAGE <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 16:04:03 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] MIRAGE: a password change holds for the source that made it

Stock Cowrie said 'password updated successfully' and forgot it: the next
login with the new password was judged by the same static list as any other
and usually refused. Whoever changes a password and logs in again to check
saw a host that said one thing and did another (found by the depth
baseline's two rotation scenarios, 16 November 2026).

A change that is told as done is now remembered for the address it came from
(cowrie/mirage_credentials.py). That source's later logins to that account
succeed with the new password and with nothing else. Every other source, and
every other account, is judged as before: a change is not a way to open an
account to the world. The store is in memory, bounded, and keeps a keyed
digest, not the password.

passwd and chpasswd answer as Debian 12's shadow 4.13 does (prompts, the
three failures, an account that does not exist, a user who is not root),
from a recording of the reference host, and the account's entry in the
session's /etc/shadow changes with it.
